      {
        "tipo": "gramatica",
        "min": 8,
        "titulo": "Verbs with -ся: the action comes back to the doer",
        "explicacao": "Some verbs end in -ся, such as «просыпаться» (to wake up) and «умываться» (to wash oneself). The action comes back to the person who does it, like \"to get oneself up\". English often leaves this out (\"I wash\", \"I wake up\"), or uses \"myself\", but Russian marks it on the verb. Conjugate normally and add -сь if the form ends in a vowel (я просыпаюсь, вы просыпаетесь) or -ся if it ends in a consonant (он просыпается, они просыпаются). «Вставать» (to get up) follows the same pattern without -ся: я встаю.",
        "exemplos": [
          {
            "ru": "Я встаю в семь часов.",
            "pt": "I get up at seven o'clock."
          },
